{% doc %}
  Emits padding as custom properties, for a section or for a block.

  ## Class or attribute — both work

  `base.css` matches either:

      .section-spacing        /  .block-spacing         write it yourself
      [data-section-spacing]  /  [data-block-spacing]   emitted below

  Reach for the class in new markup. This snippet keeps emitting the attribute
  because it cannot merge a class into an element that already has a `class`
  attribute — two `class` attributes on one tag and the browser takes the first,
  silently dropping every other class on the section root.

  Neither hook involves JavaScript. `[data-*]` in a stylesheet is an ordinary
  attribute selector: same cascade, same cost as a class, nothing reading it at
  runtime.

  ## The hook is the whole point

  `base.css` applies this padding through attribute selectors:

      [data-section-spacing] {
        padding-block-start: var(--section-padding-block-start, 0px);
        padding-block-end:   var(--section-padding-block-end, 0px);
      }

  This snippet emitted only the `style` attribute. The custom properties landed
  on elements that no rule ever selected, so every padding slider in the theme
  editor moved a value nothing read — present in the UI, dead on the page, in
  sections and blocks alike. The attribute is what connects them.

  ## Two property sets, not one

  A section pads block-start and block-end and takes an explicit mobile
  override. A block pads all four sides and, on small screens, either scales the
  desktop value by a percentage or takes an explicit per-side mobile value where
  the block offers one. Different shapes, so different property names, matching
  what `base.css` reads:

      --section-padding-block-start / -end          (+ `-mobile` variants)
      --block-padding-block-start / -end / -inline-start / -inline-end
      --block-padding-scale-mobile

  ## Truthiness, not blank comparisons

  Every guard here is either an `if object` test or a number coerced with
  `| default: 0 | plus: 0`. Neither depends on how a Liquid engine compares an
  absent value to `blank` — and that comparison is exactly where this went
  wrong before: an unset `padding_top_mobile` passed the blank test and emitted
  `--section-padding-block-start-mobile:px`, an invalid declaration that takes
  the whole rule down with it.

  A hash is truthy and nil is falsy in every Liquid — but truthiness alone is
  not enough here, because `section` is a global object and is therefore truthy
  inside every block too. See the note on `section.settings` below.

  ## A slider that exists is written, including at zero

  This used to skip the declaration whenever the value was zero, on the grounds
  that the fallbacks in `base.css` already resolve to `0px` and writing `0px`
  restates the stylesheet. Both halves of that were true and the conclusion was
  still wrong, because a fallback only applies when the property is set nowhere
  — and there are two places it can be set without this snippet writing it:

      inherited   custom properties inherit, and blocks nest four deep in this
                  theme. A row inside an accordion inside a tab inside a group
                  with padding on it inherited that padding, so dragging the
                  row's own slider to 0 gave it the group's value instead.

      a component `.block-text--has-background` and `.block-subheading--bordered`
                  set these properties as class-level defaults, so a background
                  gets breathing room without the merchant going to find the
                  sliders. At zero the class won and 0 was unreachable.

  So the rule is presence, not value: if the block has the setting at all, its
  value is written, and `0px` is a value. A block with no padding settings
  writes nothing and the class defaults still apply — `base.css` keeps them in a
  `max()` beside the emitted value, which is what makes both true at once.

  Presence is an `if` on the setting rather than a `!= blank` test: nil is falsy
  in every Liquid engine and **0 is truthy**, which is exactly the distinction
  needed and exactly the one `blank` cannot make.

  A block's mobile sides follow the same rule, for the same reason: they inherit
  too, and a heading inside a card with 18px of mobile padding was given 18px of
  its own the moment its own slider read 0. Nothing is lost by writing them —
  the "leave at 0 and the desktop value is scaled" path belongs to blocks that
  offer **Mobile padding** as a percentage instead of per-side fields, and no
  block in the theme offers both. A block with no mobile fields still writes
  none, so that path is untouched.

  Sections keep it: their mobile fields say "Leave at 0 to use 60% of the
  desktop value" on the setting itself, sections do not nest, and nothing
  inherits `--section-padding-*` from anywhere.

  Left and right are spelled `inline-start` and `inline-end` so they follow the
  text direction and flip in right-to-left languages.

  ## `declarations: true` — properties only, no attributes

  By default this emits its own `style` attribute. An element that already needs
  a `style` attribute of its own therefore ends up with two, and a browser keeps
  the first and silently drops the second — which is how the padding sliders on
  the text and image blocks moved a value that never reached the page.

  Passing `declarations: true` returns the bare declaration list instead, so the
  caller can concatenate it into its single attribute. The caller then writes
  `data-block-spacing` itself, because the hook is what `base.css` selects on.

  @param {object} [section] - A section settings object
  @param {object} [block] - A block settings object
  @param {boolean} [declarations] - Return properties only, no `style=` and no `data-*` hook. Defaults to false.

  Pass one or the other. `settings` is not accepted — it is a global drop in
  Shopify, so a snippet sees it whether or not the caller passed it, and using
  it as a fallback made every section claim to be a block as well.

  @example
    <div {% render 'spacing', block: block.settings %}>

  @example
    <section {% render 'spacing', section: section.settings %}>
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  comment
    `settings` is deliberately NOT accepted as an alias for `block`.

    In Shopify Liquid, `settings` is a global drop — the theme's own settings —
    and a snippet sees globals even when the caller passes nothing. So
    `block | default: settings` never fell through to nil on a section call: it
    fell through to the *theme settings object*, which is always truthy. Every
    section root in the theme came out with both hooks on it:

      class="announcement-bar section-spacing …"
      data-section-spacing data-block-spacing

    and the stray one is not cosmetic. `[data-block-spacing]` sets all four
    paddings to `var(--block-padding-*, 0px)`, so it overrode whatever padding
    the section's own stylesheet gave it with zero — on every section on the
    page. The one caller that used the alias, `snippets/group.liquid`, now
    passes `block:` instead.

    The two scopes are mutually exclusive by construction below rather than by
    the caller remembering to pass only one.
  endcomment
  comment
    `section` is a GLOBAL object in Shopify, not just a parameter.

    A snippet rendered from inside a section sees it whether or not the caller
    passed it, so `if section` was true for every block on the page and every
    block came out carrying `data-section-spacing` as well as its own hook. The
    section rule then applied `--section-padding-*` to a block, which resolves
    to `0px` and flattened whatever padding the block's own stylesheet gave it.

    Exactly the bug the `settings` alias caused, from exactly the same cause:
    a parameter name that collides with a global.

    The discriminator is `section.settings`. A real Shopify `section` object has
    it; a settings hash passed as `section:` does not, because a setting cannot
    be named `settings`. So the global leaking in is distinguishable from a
    caller genuinely passing one, without renaming the parameter at sixty call
    sites.
  endcomment
  assign section_is_global = false
  if section.settings
    assign section_is_global = true
  endif

  assign is_section = false
  assign is_block = false

  comment
    Block wins when both are present, which is the case inside every block: the
    caller passed `block:` and the global `section` came along for the ride.
  endcomment
  if block
    assign is_block = true
  elsif section and section_is_global == false
    assign is_section = true
  endif

  assign b = block

  assign out = ''

  if is_section
    assign s_top = section.padding_top | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign s_bottom = section.padding_bottom | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign s_top_mobile = section.padding_top_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign s_bottom_mobile = section.padding_bottom_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0

    comment
      Presence, not value. 0 is truthy in Liquid and nil is not, so this is true
      for a slider dragged to zero and false for a section that has no such
      setting — which is the difference that decides whether 0 is a value or an
      absence.
    endcomment
    assign has_s_top = false
    if section.padding_top
      assign has_s_top = true
    endif
    assign has_s_bottom = false
    if section.padding_bottom
      assign has_s_bottom = true
    endif

    if has_s_top
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-start:' | append: s_top | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if has_s_bottom
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-end:' | append: s_bottom | append: 'px;'
    endif

    comment
      Mobile is only emitted when the merchant set it. Left at 0 the stylesheet
      falls back to the desktop value, which is what the "leave at 0 to use the
      desktop value" hint on those settings promises.
    endcomment
    if s_top_mobile > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-start-mobile:' | append: s_top_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if s_bottom_mobile > 0
      assign out = out | append: '--section-padding-block-end-mobile:' | append: s_bottom_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
  endif

  if is_block
    comment
      Both spellings are accepted. Most blocks use padding_top; a few newer ones
      use the CSS-shaped padding-block-start. Reading either means a block does
      not have to be rewritten to gain working padding.

      The default filter does not replace 0, only nil, false and empty string —
      so a merchant's deliberate 0 survives the chain rather than falling
      through to the other spelling.
    endcomment
    assign b_top = b.padding_top | default: b['padding-block-start'] | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_bottom = b.padding_bottom | default: b['padding-block-end'] | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_left = b.padding_left | default: b['padding-inline-start'] | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_right = b.padding_right | default: b['padding-inline-end'] | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_scale = b.padding_scale_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0

    comment
      Presence, not value — see the note at the top. Either spelling counts as
      having the setting, and 0 counts as having a value.
    endcomment
    assign has_b_top = false
    if b.padding_top or b['padding-block-start']
      assign has_b_top = true
    endif
    assign has_b_bottom = false
    if b.padding_bottom or b['padding-block-end']
      assign has_b_bottom = true
    endif
    assign has_b_left = false
    if b.padding_left or b['padding-inline-start']
      assign has_b_left = true
    endif
    assign has_b_right = false
    if b.padding_right or b['padding-inline-end']
      assign has_b_right = true
    endif

    if has_b_top
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-block-start:' | append: b_top | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if has_b_bottom
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-block-end:' | append: b_bottom | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if has_b_left
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-inline-start:' | append: b_left | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if has_b_right
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-inline-end:' | append: b_right | append: 'px;'
    endif

    comment
      Per-device padding. A block that offers a Desktop/Mobile switch writes
      `padding_top_mobile` and friends, and `base.css` prefers those over the
      scaled desktop value. A block that does not offer them emits nothing here,
      so the scale below stays in charge — which is what every existing block
      relies on.
    endcomment
    assign b_top_mobile = b.padding_top_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_bottom_mobile = b.padding_bottom_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_left_mobile = b.padding_left_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign b_right_mobile = b.padding_right_mobile | default: 0 | plus: 0

    assign has_b_top_mobile = false
    if b.padding_top_mobile
      assign has_b_top_mobile = true
    endif
    assign has_b_bottom_mobile = false
    if b.padding_bottom_mobile
      assign has_b_bottom_mobile = true
    endif
    assign has_b_left_mobile = false
    if b.padding_left_mobile
      assign has_b_left_mobile = true
    endif
    assign has_b_right_mobile = false
    if b.padding_right_mobile
      assign has_b_right_mobile = true
    endif

    if has_b_top_mobile
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-block-start-mobile:' | append: b_top_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if has_b_bottom_mobile
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-block-end-mobile:' | append: b_bottom_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if has_b_left_mobile
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-inline-start-mobile:' | append: b_left_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif
    if has_b_right_mobile
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-inline-end-mobile:' | append: b_right_mobile | append: 'px;'
    endif

    comment
      The setting is a percentage; the stylesheet multiplies by it, so it has to
      arrive as a unitless fraction.
    endcomment
    if b_scale > 0
      assign scale = b_scale | divided_by: 100.0
      assign out = out | append: '--block-padding-scale-mobile:' | append: scale | append: ';'
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if declarations -%}
  {{- out -}}
{%- else -%}
  {%- if is_section -%}data-section-spacing {% endif -%}
  {%- if is_block -%}data-block-spacing {% endif -%}
  {%- if out != blank %}style="{{ out }}"{% endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
